</figure>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, prior methods that elicit adversarial hallucinations fail to generate realistic attacks. For example, token-level optimization methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib90" title="" class="ltx_ref">90</a>, <a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">81</a>)</cite> often produce unnatural or incoherent prompts (e.g., <a href="#S1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 1</span></a>(b)). Also, LLM agent-based <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>)</cite>, beam search-based <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>)</cite>, and manual prompting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">75</a>, <a href="#bib.bib85" title="" class="ltx_ref">85</a>)</cite> approaches generate fluent prompts that frequently diverge from the original question and are not semantically equivalent (e.g., <a href="#S1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 1</span></a>(c),(d)). Such prompts provide limited insight into how hallucinations may occur in realistic scenarios and limited value for assessing LLM robustness.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To generate realistic attacks for hallucination elicitation, we pose the following research question:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S1.p4.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="57.23" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 57.23" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,57.23) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#404040;" fill="#404040" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 0 L 0 57.23 L 477.38 57.23 L 477.38 0 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 1.97 L 1.97 55.26 L 475.41 55.26 L 475.41 1.97 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 16.47)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:30.69em;--ltx-fo-height:1.95em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="29.67" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 26.98)" width="424.66"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S1.p4.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:30.69em;">
<span id="S1.p4.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p4.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(Q1) How can we formulate the problem of generating realistic attacks for hallucination elicitation as an optimization problem?</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Realistic adversarial attacks have been widely explored in computer vision (CV) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>, <a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">71</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib88" title="" class="ltx_ref">88</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>)</cite>,
where the class of the adversarially perturbed image <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> is the same as that of the original image <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to a human observer,
yet <math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> causes a target model to produce a misclassification. Such attacks can be found via solving the optimization problem (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), where the objective is to maximize the classification loss <math id="S1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{cls}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>cls</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{cls}}</annotation></semantics></math> subject to two constraints: (i) the adversarial image <math id="S1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> must remain close to the original image <math id="S1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, that is, <math id="S1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\text{img}}(\bm{x},\bm{x}_{0})\leq\epsilon_{\text{img}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>img</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>img</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\text{img}}(\bm{x},\bm{x}_{0})\leq\epsilon_{\text{img}}</annotation></semantics></math>; and (ii) <math id="S1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> must lie within the set of valid images <math id="S1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{\text{img}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mtext>img</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{\text{img}}</annotation></semantics></math>, e.g., staying within the valid pixel range and resembling a natural-looking input.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Inspired by problem (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), we formulate realistic attacks for hallucination elicitation in LLMs as problem (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>): the objective is to maximize the hallucination loss <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{hall}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>hall</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{hall}}</annotation></semantics></math>, subject to two constraints: (i) the adversarial prompt <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> must semantically close to the original prompt <math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, namely <math id="S1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\text{text}}(\bm{x},\bm{x}_{0})\leq\epsilon_{\text{text}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>text</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>text</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\text{text}}(\bm{x},\bm{x}_{0})\leq\epsilon_{\text{text}}</annotation></semantics></math>; and (ii) <math id="S1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> must belong to the set of valid prompts <math id="S1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{\text{text}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mtext>text</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{\text{text}}</annotation></semantics></math>. In both cases, the attack objectives are targeted: the output of <math id="S1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\text{CV}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>CV</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\text{CV}}</annotation></semantics></math> is driven towards the target image class <math id="S1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}^{*}_{\text{img}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒚</mi><mtext>img</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}^{*}_{\text{img}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the output of <math id="S1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\text{LLM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>LLM</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\text{LLM}}</annotation></semantics></math> towards the target hallucination prompt <math id="S1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}^{*}_{\text{text}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒚</mi><mtext>text</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}^{*}_{\text{text}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The distances <math id="S1.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\text{img}}(\cdot,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>img</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\text{img}}(\cdot,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\text{text}}(\cdot,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>text</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\text{text}}(\cdot,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> ensure proximity under budgets <math id="S1.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\text{img}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>img</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\text{img}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\text{text}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>text</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\text{text}}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:155.3pt;">
<table id="S1.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}\max_{\bm{x}}&amp;\quad\mathcal{L}_{\text{cls}}\left(f_{\text{CV}}(\bm{x}),\bm{y}^{*}_{\text{img}}\right),\\
\text{s.t. }&amp;\quad d_{\text{img}}(\bm{x},\bm{x}_{0})\leq\epsilon_{\text{img}},\\
&amp;\quad\bm{x}\in\mathcal{X}_{\text{img}}.\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>𝒙</mi></munder></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>cls</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>CV</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒚</mi><mtext>img</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mtext>s.t. </mtext></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>img</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>img</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mtext>img</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}\max_{\bm{x}}&amp;\quad\mathcal{L}_{\text{cls}}\left(f_{\text{CV}}(\bm{x}),\bm{y}^{*}_{\text{img}}\right),\\
\text{s.t. }&amp;\quad d_{\text{img}}(\bm{x},\bm{x}_{0})\leq\epsilon_{\text{img}},\\
&amp;\quad\bm{x}\in\mathcal{X}_{\text{img}}.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:155.3pt;">
<table id="S1.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}\max_{\bm{x}}&amp;\quad\mathcal{L}_{\text{hall}}\left(f_{\text{LLM}}(\bm{x}),\bm{y}^{*}_{\text{text}}\right),\\
\text{s.t. }&amp;\quad d_{\text{text}}(\bm{x},\bm{x}_{0})\leq\epsilon_{\text{text}},\\
&amp;\quad\bm{x}\in\mathcal{X}_{\text{text}}.\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>𝒙</mi></munder></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>hall</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>LLM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒚</mi><mtext>text</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mtext>s.t. </mtext></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>text</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>text</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mtext>text</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}\max_{\bm{x}}&amp;\quad\mathcal{L}_{\text{hall}}\left(f_{\text{LLM}}(\bm{x}),\bm{y}^{*}_{\text{text}}\right),\\
\text{s.t. }&amp;\quad d_{\text{text}}(\bm{x},\bm{x}_{0})\leq\epsilon_{\text{text}},\\
&amp;\quad\bm{x}\in\mathcal{X}_{\text{text}}.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">This formulation raises a question: how should we define the proximity and validity constraints in the discrete prompt space? For the proximity constraint, prior work has primarily relied on semantic similarity measures <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">79</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">82</a>, <a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">80</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>)</cite>, but similarity alone is insufficient for modeling realistic attacks. For instance, prompts “What is the value of <math id="S1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="24=2p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>24</mn><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">24=2p</annotation></semantics></math>?” and “What is the value of <math id="S1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="24=3p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>24</mn><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">24=3p</annotation></semantics></math>?” would be judged as semantically similar, yet they differ substantially in task goal and lead to different correct answers. By contrast, <span id="S1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">semantic equivalence</span> provides a more appropriate notion of proximity. In program or formal expression domains <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>, <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>)</cite>, it refers to entities that produce the same result despite surface differences. In natural language <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>, <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>)</cite>, it refers to mutual entailment (i.e., logical implication) between two prompts. We therefore define the proximity constraint <math id="S1.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\text{text}}(\bm{x},\bm{x}_{0})\leq\epsilon_{\text{text}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>text</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>text</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\text{text}}(\bm{x},\bm{x}_{0})\leq\epsilon_{\text{text}}</annotation></semantics></math> in problem (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) as a <span id="S1.p9.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">semantic equivalence</span> constraint. For the validity constraint, the definition is more straightforward, since it is commonly understood as requiring semantically coherent and human-like language <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">84</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>)</cite>. We therefore define the validity constraint <math id="S1.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}\in\mathcal{X}_{\text{text}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mtext>text</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}\in\mathcal{X}_{\text{text}}</annotation></semantics></math> as a <span id="S1.p9.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">semantic coherence</span> constraint.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">With both constraints defined, we characterize our problem (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) as problem (<a href="#S1.E3" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), seeking <span id="S1.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">semantically equivalent</span> and <span id="S1.p10.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">coherent</span> adversarial prompts to elicit LLM hallucinations.</p>
<table id="S1.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\max_{\bm{x}}\quad\mathcal{L}_{\text{hall}}\left(f_{\text{LLM}}(\bm{x}),\bm{y}^{*}_{\text{text}}\right)\qquad\text{s.t.}\qquad\text{SE}(\bm{x},\bm{x}_{0})=1\quad\text{and}\quad\text{SC}(\bm{x})\leq\gamma." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>𝒙</mi></munder><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>hall</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>LLM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒚</mi><mtext>text</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>SE</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>SC</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\max_{\bm{x}}\quad\mathcal{L}_{\text{hall}}\left(f_{\text{LLM}}(\bm{x}),\bm{y}^{*}_{\text{text}}\right)\qquad\text{s.t.}\qquad\text{SE}(\bm{x},\bm{x}_{0})=1\quad\text{and}\quad\text{SC}(\bm{x})\leq\gamma.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p10.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{SE}(\cdot,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>SE</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{SE}(\cdot,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is a binary function that equals <math id="S1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> if two prompts are semantically equivalent and <math id="S1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise, while <math id="S1.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{SC}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>SC</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{SC}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> quantifies the degree of semantic coherence of a prompt (lower values indicate greater semantic coherence) with threshold <math id="S1.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we pose the following research question:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p12" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S1.p12.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="57.23" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 57.23" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,57.23) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#404040;" fill="#404040" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 0 L 0 57.23 L 477.38 57.23 L 477.38 0 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 1.97 L 1.97 55.26 L 475.41 55.26 L 475.41 1.97 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 16.47)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:30.69em;--ltx-fo-height:1.95em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="29.67" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 26.98)" width="424.66"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S1.p12.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:30.69em;">
<span id="S1.p12.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p12.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(Q2) How can we solve (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) to obtain semantically equivalent and coherent prompts that elicit LLM hallucinations?</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Addressing Q2 requires overcoming two key challenges. First, exploring the discrete prompt space is combinatorially hard <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib90" title="" class="ltx_ref">90</a>)</cite>. Second, because frontier LLMs already achieve high accuracy on many benchmarks, hallucination-inducing rephrasings are expected to be rare and thus difficult to uncover without exhaustive search. To tackle these challenges, we introduce Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks (SECA), which avoids the need for exhaustive search over a combinatorial space by leveraging LLMs to propose and enforce feasible rephrasings. An illustrative example of SECA is shown in  <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2.1 Problem Formulation ‣ 2 Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks (SECA) ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 1</span></a>. The key contributions of our approach are summarized as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In  <a href="#S1" title="1 Introduction ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref">§<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></span></a> and <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 Problem Formulation ‣ 2 Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks (SECA) ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref">§<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></span></a>, we formalize the problem of generating <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">semantically equivalent</span> and <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">coherent</span> attacks for hallucination elicitation in LLMs as a constrained optimization problem.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 SECA: Traversing the Space of Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Prompts ‣ 2 Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks (SECA) ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref">§<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></span></a>, we propose SECA, a constraint-preserving zeroth-order method that effectively identifies the most adversarial yet feasible prompts in a gradient-free manner.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#S3" title="3 Experiments ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref">§<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></span></a>, we demonstrate SECA’s effectiveness on open-ended multiple-choice question answering tasks across frontier open-source and commercial LLMs, showing its ability to generate diverse, semantically equivalent, and coherent prompts that successfully elicit factual and faithful hallucinations. We also show strong agreement between LLM-based evaluators and human annotators, validating the reliability of automated evaluation in this setting. Moreover, we analyze the characteristics of successful attacks, finding that more verbose and lexically diverse prompts are more likely to induce hallucinations. Overall, our work underscores the importance of evaluating LLM robustness under realistic attacks.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks (SECA)</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Problem Formulation</h3>

<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:39%;"><img src="2510.04398v1/illustrative_figs_tabs/teaser_figure_revised.png" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:191/116;" width="191" height="116" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S2.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
Our SECA finds semantically equivalent and coherent attacks to elicit LLM hallucinations. See Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Example of SECA Attack Adversarial Prompt ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref">§<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></span></a> for a detailed example.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#S1" title="1 Introduction ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref">§<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></span></a>, we introduced the general constrained optimization problem (<a href="#S1.E3" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) for the semantically equivalent and coherent attacks. We now provide a more detailed explanation. As the name suggests, our formulation consists of three components, which we present in the sequel: the attack (objective), semantic equivalence (constraint), and coherence (constraint).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Attack</span>. Prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib90" title="" class="ltx_ref">90</a>)</cite> showed that forcing aligned LLMs to produce certain tokens can induce harmful behavior. Inspired by this, given an input prompt and a set of answer choices, our goal is to elicit an incorrect option and a hallucinated rationale from the target LLM. We define the hallucination loss as the likelihood of generating a target response <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> given the user prompt <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle P\left(\bm{y}^{*}\mid\bm{x}\right)=P(\bm{y}_{1}^{*}\mid\bm{x})\cdot\prod_{t=2}^{T}P(\bm{y}_{t}^{*}\mid\bm{x},\bm{y}^{*}_{1:t-1})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒚</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover></mstyle><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P\left(\bm{y}^{*}\mid\bm{x}\right)=P(\bm{y}_{1}^{*}\mid\bm{x})\cdot\prod_{t=2}^{T}P(\bm{y}_{t}^{*}\mid\bm{x},\bm{y}^{*}_{1:t-1}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is the total number of tokens in the target response <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}^{*}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}^{*}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-th token in the target response, and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P(\bm{y}^{*}_{t}\mid\bm{x},\bm{y}^{*}_{1:t-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\bm{y}^{*}_{t}\mid\bm{x},\bm{y}^{*}_{1:t-1})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the probability of generating <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}^{*}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}^{*}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> given the input prompt <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and tokens <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bm{y}^{*}_{1:t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}^{*}_{1:t-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. For instance, <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> can be the multiple-choice question in <a href="#S1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 1</span></a><span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            In our experiments, the full input is obtained by inserting the question <math id="footnote1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> into a fixed template. Please see Appendix <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Example Full Prompt to A Target LLM ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref">§<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></span></a> for the complete template.</span></span></span>, and the target response <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to a single token associated with the factuality-hallucinated choice, e.g., <span id="S2.SS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">"B"</span>. Our experiments in <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Empirical Analysis of SECA ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref">§<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></span></a> show that responses beginning with an incorrect token are strongly associated with hallucinated reasoning in the model’s explanation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Semantic Equivalence</span>. In CV (see problem (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>)), adversarial attacks require adversarial images remaining visually similar to the original image under metrics such as <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> distance or perceptual similarity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>)</cite>. By analogy, in the language domain (see problems (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) and (<a href="#S1.E3" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>)), we advocate that realistic attacks should alter the original prompt only in ways that preserve semantic equivalence to humans. To explicitly characterize the semantic equivalence constraint <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{SE}(\bm{x},\bm{x}_{0})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>SE</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{SE}(\bm{x},\bm{x}_{0})=1</annotation></semantics></math> introduced in problem (<a href="#S1.E3" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), we use a binary <span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">feasibility checker</span> <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{LLM}_{\texttt{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">LLM</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">F</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{LLM}_{\texttt{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> that determines whether two prompts satisfy semantic equivalence (see Appendix <a href="#A8" title="Appendix H Instruction Prompt for Feasibility Checker ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref">§<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H</span></span></a> for the instruction template). The checker evaluates <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> based on five criteria: (i) mutual entailment, (ii) <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> introduces no additional information beyond <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and the answer choices, (iii) <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> does not omit essential information from <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, (iv) <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> preserves the meaning of <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, and (v) <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> yields the same ground-truth answer as <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
            To reduce the difficulty of evaluating semantic equivalence, we provide the ground-truth answer of the original question to the feasibility checker.</span></span></span>. The constraint is satisfied only if all five conditions hold, i.e., <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{LLM}_{\texttt{F}}(\bm{x},\bm{x}_{0})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">LLM</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">F</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{LLM}_{\texttt{F}}(\bm{x},\bm{x}_{0})=1</annotation></semantics></math>, and is violated otherwise, i.e., <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{LLM}_{\texttt{F}}(\bm{x},\bm{x}_{0})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">LLM</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">F</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{LLM}_{\texttt{F}}(\bm{x},\bm{x}_{0})=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Semantic Coherence</span>. In CV (see problem (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>)), adversarial attacks require adversarial images to remain within the set of valid images, e.g., within the proper pixel range <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>)</cite> and resembling a natural input <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib88" title="" class="ltx_ref">88</a>)</cite>. By analogy, in the language domain (see problem (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) and (<a href="#S1.E3" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>)), we advocate that a realistic attack prompt should lie within the set of valid prompts, e.g., it should be semantically coherent to humans. A standard way to formalize the semantic coherence constraint <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{SC}(\bm{x})\leq\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>SC</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{SC}(\bm{x})\leq\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> introduced in problem (<a href="#S1.E3" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) is via perplexity, i.e., <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\text{PPL}(\bm{x})=\exp\left\{-\frac{1}{n}\sum_{t=2}^{n}\log P\left(\bm{x}_{t}|\bm{x}_{1:t-1}\right)\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>PPL</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo>{</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mi>log</mi><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{PPL}(\bm{x})=\exp\left\{-\frac{1}{n}\sum_{t=2}^{n}\log P\left(\bm{x}_{t}|\bm{x}_{1:t-1}\right)\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{PPL}(\bm{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>PPL</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{PPL}(\bm{x})</annotation></semantics></math> is non-negative, and a lower value indicates <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> is more coherent. In general, prompts that are semantically equivalent to a semantically coherent prompt are themselves coherent. However, corner cases exist: for instance, a few typos or extra characters may make a sentence incoherent, while humans remain robust to such minor perturbations and can still extract the same meaning (e.g., the ‘<math id="S2.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{SE}\setminus\text{SC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>SE</mtext><mo>∖</mo><mtext>SC</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{SE}\setminus\text{SC}</annotation></semantics></math>’ example in <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2.2 SECA: Traversing the Space of Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Prompts ‣ 2 Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks (SECA) ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 2</span></a>). Thus, an explicit semantic coherence constraint remains necessary.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Putting It All Together</span>. For a prompt <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> to be considered an attack that is both semantically equivalent to the original prompt <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and semantically coherent, we explicitly characterize problem (<a href="#S1.E3" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) as the following optimization problem:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\max_{\bm{x}}\quad\log P\left(\bm{y}^{*}\mid\bm{x}\right)\qquad\text{s.t. }\qquad\texttt{LLM}_{\texttt{F}}(\bm{x},\bm{x}_{0})=1\quad\text{and}\quad\text{PPL}(\bm{x})\leq\gamma." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>𝒙</mi></munder><mspace style="width:1.334em;" width="1.334em"></mspace><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mtext>s.t. </mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">LLM</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">F</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>PPL</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\max_{\bm{x}}\quad\log P\left(\bm{y}^{*}\mid\bm{x}\right)\qquad\text{s.t. }\qquad\texttt{LLM}_{\texttt{F}}(\bm{x},\bm{x}_{0})=1\quad\text{and}\quad\text{PPL}(\bm{x})\leq\gamma.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is a hyperparameter, denoting the maximum allowable incoherence. By maximizing the log likelihood of generating the target response <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> under these constraints, the resulting prompt is expected to be adversarial, yet semantically equivalent to <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and semantically coherent.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>SECA: Traversing the Space of Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Prompts</h3>

<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:44%;"><img src="2510.04398v1/data/figs/seca_demo_revised.png" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:215/98;" width="215" height="98" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S2.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
Starting from the current prompt <math id="S2.F2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, SECA generates the next prompt <math id="S2.F2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math> while enforcing semantic equivalence and coherence constraints.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A canonical challenge in token-level optimization, which also arises in (<a href="#S2.E5" title="In 2.1 Problem Formulation ‣ 2 Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks (SECA) ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>), is that the prompt space is discrete and exponentially large. Our formulation alleviates this issue by imposing constraints on the search space. Specifically, our method traverses the space of semantically equivalent and coherent prompts, which is appreciably smaller than the entire search space. However, this introduces its own difficulties, as these constraints are difficult to explicitly enforce via classical constrained optimization techniques such as standard projection operations. To address this, our key idea is to enforce the constraints directly by leveraging LLMs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Traversing the Prompt Space with a Semantic Equivalence Proposer</span>. We feed instructions to a <span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">proposer</span> <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{LLM}_{\texttt{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">LLM</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">P</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{LLM}_{\texttt{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>, asking it to propose <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> prompts that are all semantically equivalent to a given prompt; it corresponds to Line 6 of Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 2.2 SECA: Traversing the Space of Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Prompts ‣ 2 Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks (SECA) ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> (see Appendix <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Instruction Prompt for Semantic Equivalence Proposer ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref">§<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></span></a> for the instruction template). A potential issue here is <span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">semantic collapse</span>: The <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> prompts might be identical to each other or to the original prompt. To alleviate this, we carefully inject randomness into the instructions and allow them to vary in style and in content. On the other hand, as LLMs are trained on human-written corpora, coherence surfaces as an emergent property of the LLM outputs given our instructions. Thus, we do not explicitly encourage the proposer to produce coherent responses.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks (SECA)  </figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> Original prompt <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, target prompt <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, proposer <math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{LLM}_{\texttt{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">LLM</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">P</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{LLM}_{\texttt{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>; feasibility checker <math id="alg1.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{LLM}_{\texttt{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">LLM</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">F</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{LLM}_{\texttt{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Initialize:</span> <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{\text{best}}\leftarrow\bm{x}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mtext>best</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{\text{best}}\leftarrow\bm{x}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, an array of <span id="alg1.l2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">candidates</span> <math id="alg1.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\bm{x}_{0}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\bm{x}_{0}]</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Terminology</span>: We say <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{a}</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="alg1.l3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">more adversarial</span> than <math id="alg1.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒃</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{b}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="alg1.l3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log P\left(\bm{y}^{*}\mid\bm{a}\right)&gt;\log P\left(\bm{y}^{*}\mid\bm{b}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝒃</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log P\left(\bm{y}^{*}\mid\bm{a}\right)&gt;\log P\left(\bm{y}^{*}\mid\bm{b}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>
<span id="alg1.l3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">while</span> stop criterion not reached:

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              
               <span id="alg1.l4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">candidates_tmp</span> <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">candidates</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
              
               <span id="alg1.l5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> each <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> in <span id="alg1.l5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">candidates</span>:

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
              
                 <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\bm{x}_{i}]_{i=1}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\bm{x}_{i}]_{i=1}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="alg1.l6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="alg1.l6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> prompts proposed by <math id="alg1.l6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{LLM}_{\texttt{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">LLM</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">P</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{LLM}_{\texttt{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> given <math id="alg1.l6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and instructions;

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
              
              
                 <span id="alg1.l7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">feasible</span> <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math> prompts in <math id="alg1.l7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\bm{x}_{i}]_{i=1}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\bm{x}_{i}]_{i=1}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math> that pass the feasibility test of <math id="alg1.l7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{LLM}_{\texttt{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">LLM</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">F</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{LLM}_{\texttt{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>; <span id="alg1.l7.4" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg1.l7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> see <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 SECA: Traversing the Space of Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Prompts ‣ 2 Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks (SECA) ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref">§<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></span></a>
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
              
              
                 <span id="alg1.l8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">adv</span> <math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math> all prompts in <span id="alg1.l8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">feasible</span> that are more adversarial than <math id="alg1.l8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math>; 
</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
              
              
                 <span id="alg1.l9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">candidates_tmp</span> <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math> merge <span id="alg1.l9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">candidates_tmp</span> and <span id="alg1.l9.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">adv</span>;

</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
              
              
               <span id="alg1.l10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">candidates</span> <math id="alg1.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the most <math id="alg1.l10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> adversarial</span> prompts in <span id="alg1.l10.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">candidates_tmp</span>;

</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
              
              
               <math id="alg1.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{\text{best}}\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mtext>best</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{\text{best}}\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the most adversarial</span> prompt in <span id="alg1.l11.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">candidates</span>;

</div>
<div id="alg1.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">12:</span></span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output:</span> <math id="alg1.l12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{\text{best}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mtext>best</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{\text{best}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Enforcing Constraints via a Feasibility Checker</span>. In principle, all candidate prompts generated by <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{LLM}_{\texttt{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">LLM</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">P</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{LLM}_{\texttt{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> should be semantically equivalent to the input <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math>. In practice, however, the auxiliary LLM may still produce hallucinations. To mitigate this, we verify each candidate using the <span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">feasibility checker</span> <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{LLM}_{\texttt{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">LLM</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">F</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{LLM}_{\texttt{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> from problem (<a href="#S2.E5" title="In 2.1 Problem Formulation ‣ 2 Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks (SECA) ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>), which evaluates whether <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy the semantic equivalence constraint (see Line 7 of Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 2.2 SECA: Traversing the Space of Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Prompts ‣ 2 Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks (SECA) ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and Appendix <a href="#A8" title="Appendix H Instruction Prompt for Feasibility Checker ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref">§<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H</span></span></a> for the instruction template). This additional verification provides strict feasibility guarantees, as shown in <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Attack Performance Comparison with GCG ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref">§<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The Most Adversarial Attack</span>. With the proposer and feasibility checker in effect, we have at our disposal a limited number of candidate prompts rather than the entire space. Moreover, these prompts tend to satisfy the constraints of semantic equivalence and coherence. Thus, it only remains to discover, among candidates, the one that is <span id="S2.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the most adversarial</span>. As a proxy of the adversarial level of a prompt <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, we condition on this prompt and compute the log likelihood <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log P\left(\bm{y}^{*}\mid\bm{x}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log P\left(\bm{y}^{*}\mid\bm{x}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> of generating <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, by following the philosophy of our optimization problem (<a href="#S2.E5" title="In 2.1 Problem Formulation ‣ 2 Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks (SECA) ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>), the most adversarial attack arises as the prompt among the candidates that maximizes this log likelihood.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Putting It All Together</span>. Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 2.2 SECA: Traversing the Space of Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Prompts ‣ 2 Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks (SECA) ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> integrates the above components into a unified procedure. We begin by initializing the candidate set with <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> copies of the original prompt <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. At each iteration, new candidates are generated by the semantic equivalence proposer, filtered by the feasibility checker, and then their adversarial strengths are evaluated. Among all feasible candidates, we retain only the top-<math id="S2.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> adversarial prompts to form the next iteration (Line 10 of Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 2.2 SECA: Traversing the Space of Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Prompts ‣ 2 Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks (SECA) ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). The process terminates when either the log-likelihood <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log P\left(\bm{y}^{*}\mid\bm{x}_{\text{best}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mtext>best</mtext></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log P\left(\bm{y}^{*}\mid\bm{x}_{\text{best}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> exceeds a predefined threshold or a maximum number of iterations is reached. These design choices make SECA a constraint-preserving zeroth-order method that is simple and effective. An illustrative example of one iteration is shown in <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2.2 SECA: Traversing the Space of Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Prompts ‣ 2 Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks (SECA) ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Experimental Setups</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dataset and Target Token</span>. We evaluate our approach with the commonly used MMLU dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>)</cite>, where each sample consists of multiple-choice questions and the correct answer. However, some questions in this dataset might already induce hallucinations of a target LLM. To isolate the effect of this, we only consider the questions for which the target LLMs produce correct answers. To do so, we create a filtered subset of MMLU, where each prompt is included if and only if all target LLMs assign the highest confidence to the correct answer token. After this filtering, the resulting dataset contains 347 samples and spans 16 diverse subjects such as science, engineering, and arts<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
              
              
              
            See Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C List of Abbreviations ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref">§<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></span></a> for full subject list and their corresponding abbreviations</span></span></span>. Motivated by MMLU, we conduct attacks in an open-ended multiple-choice question answering (MCQA) setting. Each question is paired with four answer options labeled ‘A’, ‘B’, ‘C’, and ‘D’, which are included in the input prompt. The target LLM is then required to produce an answer choice and a corresponding explanation<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
              
              
              
            See Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Example of SECA Attack Adversarial Prompt ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref">§<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></span></a> for a detailed attack example.</span></span></span>. Within this setup, the log likelihood <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log P(\bm{y}^{*}|\bm{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log P(\bm{y}^{*}|\bm{x})</annotation></semantics></math> in the experiments measures the log probability that an input <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> elicits the target incorrect token <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, e.g., ‘B’. In practice, we designate the most likely incorrect answer choice in the MCQA as <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines</span>. Our first baseline, <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Raw</span>, simply uses the original MMLU queries directly as attacks on target LLMs. The second baseline is <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Greedy Coordinate Gradient</span> (GCG) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib90" title="" class="ltx_ref">90</a>)</cite>. We do not include other hallucination-elicitation or jailbreak baselines, as we are not aware of any prior work explicitly designed to address the task in problem (<a href="#S1.E3" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), namely finding semantically equivalent and coherent prompts (See §<a href="#S1" title="1 Introduction ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, §<a href="#S4" title="4 Related Work ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, and Appendix §<a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Comparison with Jailbreak Attacks ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a> for detailed discussion). We therefore select GCG as the most representative SOTA method applicable to hallucination elicitation. Additional configuration details for both GCG and SECA are provided in Appendix <a href="#A12" title="Appendix L Additional Experimental Setups ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref">§<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">L</span></span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLMs</span>. We use both open source and commercial models as our target LLMs; this includes <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Qwen-2.5-7B/14B, Llama2-13B, Llama3-3B/8B, GPT-4.1-Nano, GPT-4o-Mini</span> (see Appendix <a href="#A10" title="Appendix J LLM version ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref">§<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">J</span></span></a> for the detailed version of LLMs used in this paper). For SECA, we use GPT-4.1-Nano as the <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">semantic equivalence proposer</span> <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{LLM}_{\texttt{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">LLM</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">P</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{LLM}_{\texttt{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> due to its high response speed, low query cost, and strong instruction-following capabilities (see Appendix <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Instruction Prompt for Semantic Equivalence Proposer ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref">§<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></span></a>). We use GPT-4.1-Mini as the feasibility checker <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{LLM}_{\texttt{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">LLM</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">F</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{LLM}_{\texttt{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> (see Appendix <a href="#A8" title="Appendix H Instruction Prompt for Feasibility Checker ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref">§<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H</span></span></a>) to evaluate the semantic equivalence between two prompts <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{LLM}_{\texttt{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">LLM</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">F</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{LLM}_{\texttt{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> produces binary outputs and returns either 1 or 0.
To evaluate the semantic coherence of a prompt <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, we use the standard perplexity metric computed by GPT-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>)</cite>, e.g., <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\text{PPL}(\bm{x})=\exp\left\{-\frac{1}{n}\sum_{t=2}^{n}\log P\left(\bm{x}_{t}|\bm{x}_{1:t-1}\right)\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>PPL</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo>{</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mi>log</mi><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{PPL}(\bm{x})=\exp\left\{-\frac{1}{n}\sum_{t=2}^{n}\log P\left(\bm{x}_{t}|\bm{x}_{1:t-1}\right)\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, to analyze the specific types of hallucinations induced, we employ GPT-4.1 as a <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">hallucination evaluator</span> to classify the response of the target LLM into one of four categories: <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Factuality</span>, <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Faithfulness</span>, <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Other</span>, or <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">None</span>; see Appendix <a href="#A9" title="Appendix I Instruction for Hallucination Evaluator ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref">§<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></span></a> for the instruction template. <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Factuality</span> indicates the response contains false or inaccurate information; <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Faithfulness</span> denotes misrepresentation of the input prompt; <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Other</span> captures issues such as ambiguity, incompleteness, or under-informativeness; and <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">None</span> is assigned to responses that are both factually correct and faithful to the input.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metrics</span>. We define several evaluation metrics based on LLMs introduced above. First, note that the target LLM typically generates a token indicating the option it chooses, followed by an explanation. We say an attack is successful if it elicits an incorrect option and then an explanation that is classified as either <span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Factuality</span> or <span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Faithfulness</span> by the hallucination evaluator (see Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Example of SECA Attack Adversarial Prompt ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref">§<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></span></a> for an example of a successful attack). The <span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Best-of-<math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> Attack Success Rate</span>, written as ASR@<span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">K</span>, measures the percentage of samples for which at least one successful attack is found within <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> trials. Next, we define <span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">constraint violation</span> <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\text{SE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mtext>SE</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\text{SE}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\text{SC}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mtext>SC</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\text{SC}}</annotation></semantics></math> that measure the extent to which the constraints of (<a href="#S2.E5" title="In 2.1 Problem Formulation ‣ 2 Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks (SECA) ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) on semantic equivalence and coherence are respectively violated.</p>
<table id="S3.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}&amp;v_{\text{SE}}(\bm{x},\bm{x}_{0})=\left|\text{SE}(\bm{x},\bm{x}_{0})-1\right|=\left|\texttt{LLM}_{\texttt{F}}(\bm{x},\bm{x}_{0})-1\right|\in\left\{0,1\right\},\\
&amp;v_{\text{SC}}(\bm{x})=\max(\text{SC}(\bm{x})-\gamma,0)=\max(\text{PPL}(\bm{x})-\gamma,0)\in[0,\infty).\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mtext>SE</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>SE</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">LLM</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">F</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mtext>SC</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>SC</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>PPL</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}&amp;v_{\text{SE}}(\bm{x},\bm{x}_{0})=\left|\text{SE}(\bm{x},\bm{x}_{0})-1\right|=\left|\texttt{LLM}_{\texttt{F}}(\bm{x},\bm{x}_{0})-1\right|\in\left\{0,1\right\},\\
&amp;v_{\text{SC}}(\bm{x})=\max(\text{SC}(\bm{x})-\gamma,0)=\max(\text{PPL}(\bm{x})-\gamma,0)\in[0,\infty).\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\text{SE}}(\bm{x},\bm{x}_{0})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mtext>SE</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\text{SE}}(\bm{x},\bm{x}_{0})=0</annotation></semantics></math> indicates the algorithm output <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> preserves semantic equivalence, while <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\text{SE}}(\bm{x},\bm{x}_{0})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mtext>SE</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\text{SE}}(\bm{x},\bm{x}_{0})=1</annotation></semantics></math> indicates <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> deviates from what the original prompt <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> means. As for coherence violation, a lower value of <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\text{SC}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mtext>SC</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\text{SC}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> indicates better semantic coherence, and we consistently set <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=60" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>60</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=60</annotation></semantics></math> in all experiments. We then define <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{v}_{\text{SE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mtext>SE</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{v}_{\text{SE}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{v}_{\text{SC}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mtext>SC</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{v}_{\text{SC}}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, to be the corresponding constraint violation averaged over the dataset. Finally, we define <span id="S3.SS1.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Type Token Ratio</span> (<span id="S3.SS1.p4.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">TTR</span>) to be the ratio between the number of unique tokens in an output prompt and the total number of tokens (within a given window and averaged over the dataset). Therefore, a larger TTR indicates the prompts contain a more diverse set of vocabulary. Appendix <a href="#A12" title="Appendix L Additional Experimental Setups ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref">§<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">L</span></span></a> includes additional details of our experimental setups.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Attack Performance Comparison with GCG</h3>

<figure id="S3.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S3.T2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 2</span>: </span><span id="S3.T2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> Comparison of different algorithms in terms of ASR@30 and average constraint violations (<math id="S3.T2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{v}_{\text{SE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">¯</mo></mover><mtext>SE</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{v}_{\text{SE}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.T2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{v}_{\text{SC}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">¯</mo></mover><mtext>SC</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{v}_{\text{SC}}</annotation></semantics></math>). Evaluations are performed on a filtered MMLU subset across 16 MMLU subjects (see <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Experimental Setups ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations" class="ltx_ref">§<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></span></a>). Standard deviation (std) is calculated over 10,000 bootstrap samples with replacement.</span></figcaption>
